{"qid":"Q112039853","names":["Byzantine Empire"],"title":"Byzantine Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Byzantine Empire was the continuation of the eastern Roman Empire centered on Constantinople (founded 330 CE), maintaining Roman law, Greek language, and Orthodox Christianity through the medieval period. Under Justinian I (r. 527–565) it briefly reconquered North Africa, Italy, and southern Spain, while Basil II (r. 976–1025) crushed the Bulgarian Empire and extended the eastern frontier to Armenia. The catastrophic defeat at Manzikert (1071) opened Anatolia to Seljuk settlement, and the Fourth Crusade's sack of Constantinople in 1204 created a fifty-seven-year Latin interregnum; the Palaiologos dynasty restored Byzantine rule in 1261 but never recovered the lost territories. Constantinople fell to Mehmed II on 29 May 1453; the successor Despotate of the Morea held out until 1460 and the Empire of Trebizond until 1461, with some Byzantine polities in the Crimea lasting until 1475.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territorial reconstruction is relatively well attested from narrative and administrative sources for major periods, but frontier zones in the Balkans, Caucasus, and Anatolia fluctuated rapidly and mapping precision diminishes after 1204.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Byzantine Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Byzantine Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Byzantine-Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Justinian I","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Justinian-I"},{"label":"Britannica: Fall of Constantino